-- title:
--   Extending ρ to the subalgebra generated by red(T)
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be a field, $B$ a ring with a $\kappa$-algebra structure, and let $(c_i)_{i\in\iota}$ be a family, indexed by an arbitrary type $\iota$, of $\kappa$-linear maps $c_i : B \to \kappa$ which jointly separate points of $B$: if $c_i(b) = 0$ for all $i$ then $b = 0$. Let $T$ be a commutative ring and let $\mathrm{red} : T \to B$ and $\rho : T \to \kappa$ be ring homomorphisms, subject to two hypotheses: every coordinate $c_i(\mathrm{red}(t))$, for $t \in T$ and $i \in \iota$, lies in the bottom subfield $\bot$ of $\kappa$, that is, in the prime subfield; and $\rho$ kills the kernel of $\mathrm{red}$, i.e. $\mathrm{red}(t) = 0$ implies $\rho(t) = 0$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a homomorphism of $\kappa$-algebras $\chi$ from the $\kappa$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\ \kappa\ (\mathrm{range}\ \mathrm{red})$ of $B$ generated by the image of $\mathrm{red}$ to $\kappa$, such that for every $t \in T$ the value of $\chi$ at the element $\mathrm{red}(t)$ of that subalgebra equals $\rho(t)$. No injectivity or surjectivity is required of $\mathrm{red}$, and $\chi$ is not claimed to be unique.
--
--   This is a linear-disjointness statement over the prime field: the $\kappa$-linear span of $\mathrm{red}(T)$ inside $B$ admits the $\kappa$-linear extension of $\mathrm{red}(t) \mapsto \rho(t)$, because $\kappa$-linear relations among the $\mathrm{red}(t)$ are forced by relations with prime-field coefficients, the coordinates $c_i(\mathrm{red}(t))$ being prime-field valued. It is applied in the analysis of full-level modular curves, where the coordinates are $q$-expansion coefficients and $\rho$ is a character on a ring of Hecke-type operators, to produce an algebra character on the subalgebra generated by a reduction map.
-- source:
